.TH SYSTEM 3
.SH NAME
system \- issue a shell command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
int
system(string)
char *string;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B system
runs the command
.I string
with
.BR "/bin/sh \-c" ,
just as if it had been typed to the shell, and waits until it has
finished. While it waits, the program ignores the interrupt and quit
signals, so that the key that stops the command does not stop the
program as well.
.PP
Output the program has written to a stream should be flushed with
.BR fflush (3)
before system is called, or it may come out after the command's.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
The command's status, as
.BR wait (2)
gives it: its exit code times 256, plus the number of the signal that
killed it, if one did. \-1 if no process could be made; an exit code of
127 if the shell could not be run.
.SH SEE ALSO
sh(1), exec(2), fork(2), wait(2)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B system
function first appeared in Version 6 AT&T UNIX.
